Sushi Go! Terminal Card Game

An object-oriented C++ implementation of the card game Sushi Go!
Played against two opponents in a colorized terminal UI with multi-round scoring
and full support for all standard card types and combinations.

Overview:
Sushi Go! is a card drafting game where players pass hands
around the table, selecting one card per turn. The goal is to collect
combinations that score points: pairs of Tempura, triples of Sashimi,
sets of Dumplings, and Nigiri dipped in Wasabi.

This implementation faithfully recreates the full game:
- 9 cards dealt per player per round
- All card types and scoring rules from the original game

Features
- Full card set: Maki, Tempura, Sashimi, Dumpling, Nigiri (3 types), Wasabi, Pudding, Chopsticks
- Multi-round scoring: Maki and Pudding scored at end of round; final scores calculated across all 3 rounds
- Wasabi pairing logic: Correctly calculates Nigiri × 3 when preceded by Wasabi
- Winner detection: Handles ties, including tie-breaking by pudding count
- Chopsticks variant: Optional rule for swapping two cards for one

How to Play:
Compile:
clang++ -Wall sushigo.cpp game.cpp player.cpp board.cpp card.cpp vector.cpp termfuncs.cpp -o sushigo

Run:
./sushigo sushi.data true

